===== proxxx logs tail --help =====
Tail systemd journals from one or more cluster nodes.

Spawns `journalctl --follow --output short-iso …` over SSH to each selected node and merges the streams locally. Each line is tagged with the node it came from so cross-node correlation works out of the box.

Examples: proxxx logs tail                                    # every node, every unit proxxx logs tail --service pveproxy                 # one unit, all nodes proxxx logs tail --node pve-test-1 --node pve-test-2 proxxx logs tail --since "1 hour ago" --grep "OOM"  # cross-cluster OOM scan proxxx logs tail --since "1 hour ago" --no-follow   # finite window, then exit

Usage: proxxx logs tail [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --node <NODE>
          Node(s) to tail. May be repeated. Empty = every cluster node

      --grep <GREP>
          Filter lines containing this substring. Applied client-side after the per-node streams merge, so any string works — no journalctl regex escaping needed

      --service <SERVICE>
          `systemd` unit name. Passed verbatim to `journalctl --unit`. Common: `pveproxy`, `pvedaemon`, `corosync`, `pve-ha-lrm`

      --since <SINCE>
          Time window. Anything `journalctl --since` accepts: `"1 hour ago"`, `"2026-05-20 01:00:00"`, `"yesterday"`. Without it, a follow shows journalctl's last few lines and then new ones; `--no-follow` shows the last `--lines` lines

      --lines <LINES>
          Show at most the last N lines of each node's journal (`journalctl -n N`). With `--no-follow` and no `--since` it defaults to 200, so the command never dumps a whole journal

      --no-follow
          Don't follow; emit lines up to "now" then exit. Useful for a finite window scan: `--since "1 hour ago" --no-follow`

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Connection profile name

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format (only in CLI mode)

          Possible values:
          - json
          - json-envelope: JSON wrapped in a envelope carrying the producing version (audit 2026-09-09, #284)
          - table
          - plain
          
          [default: table]

      --token-secret <TOKEN_SECRET>
          API token secret. **Visible in the process listing** (`ps`, `/proc/<pid>/cmdline`) for as long as the command runs, and in shell history and CI logs. Prefer `--token-secret-file`, `PROXXX_TOKEN_SECRET`, or the OS keychain — especially for `daemon serve`, which is long-running

      --token-secret-file <PATH>
          Read the API token secret from a file (audit #283).
          
          Unlike `--token-secret` this leaves nothing in the process listing. The file must be readable by this process; trailing whitespace is trimmed. Takes precedence over `--token-secret` when both are given, because it is the safer of the two and a caller passing both most likely means the file.

      --secure
          Require Telegram 2FA for destructive operations in the TUI; refuse destructive CLI commands (exit 8)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

