===== proxxx daemon serve --help =====
Run alerts + HITL + schedule daemons under one process. SIGTERM/SIGINT stops all three cleanly.

Each can be opted out individually for operators who want to keep one of them under a separate systemd unit (e.g. schedule on a different cadence than the rest).

Examples: proxxx daemon serve                              # all three proxxx daemon serve --no-hitl                    # alerts + schedule proxxx daemon serve --schedule-interval-secs 30  # tighter scheduler

Usage: proxxx daemon serve [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --schedule-interval-secs <SCHEDULE_INTERVAL_SECS>
          Schedule "run-due" tick interval in seconds. Defaults to 60 (one tick per minute, matches the `* * * * * proxxx schedule run-due` cron pattern the scheduler was originally designed to be invoked from)
          
          [default: 60]

      --alerts-interval-secs <ALERTS_INTERVAL_SECS>
          Alerts loop poll interval in seconds. Default 30 (same default as `proxxx alerts watch`)
          
          [default: 30]

      --no-schedule
          Skip the schedule task. Useful when the operator wants per-minute scheduling under their own cron

      --no-alerts
          Skip the alerts task. Useful in dev environments where alert-noise to Telegram is unwanted. Without `[[alerts]]` rules the alerts task is not started anyway

      --no-hitl
          Skip the HITL task. Useful when running alongside an existing HITL listener process. Without a `[telegram]` section the HITL task is not started anyway

      --no-reconcile
          Skip the reconcile task (`GitOps` drift watch). It only runs when a `[profiles.X.reconcile]` section is configured; this flag force-disables it regardless

      --no-converge
          Skip the Layer 3 auto-converge step. The reconcile watch keeps DETECTING drift (logs / metrics / alerts); this disables only the unmanned mutation. Auto-converge also requires `auto_converge = true` in the profile's `[reconcile]` section — this flag force-disables it regardless. The `PROXXX_NO_CONVERGE` env var is the same kill-switch, checked per tick

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Connection profile name

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format (only in CLI mode)

          Possible values:
          - json
          - json-envelope: JSON wrapped in a envelope carrying the producing version (audit 2026-09-09, #284)
          - table
          - plain
          
          [default: table]

      --token-secret <TOKEN_SECRET>
          API token secret. **Visible in the process listing** (`ps`, `/proc/<pid>/cmdline`) for as long as the command runs, and in shell history and CI logs. Prefer `--token-secret-file`, `PROXXX_TOKEN_SECRET`, or the OS keychain — especially for `daemon serve`, which is long-running

      --token-secret-file <PATH>
          Read the API token secret from a file (audit #283).
          
          Unlike `--token-secret` this leaves nothing in the process listing. The file must be readable by this process; trailing whitespace is trimmed. Takes precedence over `--token-secret` when both are given, because it is the safer of the two and a caller passing both most likely means the file.

      --secure
          Require Telegram 2FA for destructive operations in the TUI; refuse destructive CLI commands (exit 8)

