===== proxxx upgrade-check --help =====
Pre-flight scanner for PVE major-version upgrades (e.g. `--target 9.x`). Cluster + config scan; structured findings with severity (info/warn/block) for CI gating. Exit 0 on info+warn only, 1 on any block

Usage: proxxx upgrade-check [OPTIONS] --target <TARGET>

Options:
      --target <TARGET>
          Upgrade target version (e.g. `9.1`, `9.x`, `10`). Match is prefix-based on the major; minor/patch are accepted but only the major is used for rule filtering

      --output <OUTPUT>
          Output format. `text` (default) for humans, `json` for CI
          
          [default: text]
          [possible values: text, json]

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Connection profile name

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format (only in CLI mode)

          Possible values:
          - json
          - json-envelope: JSON wrapped in a envelope carrying the producing version (audit 2026-09-09, #284)
          - table
          - plain
          
          [default: table]

      --token-secret <TOKEN_SECRET>
          API token secret. **Visible in the process listing** (`ps`, `/proc/<pid>/cmdline`) for as long as the command runs, and in shell history and CI logs. Prefer `--token-secret-file`, `PROXXX_TOKEN_SECRET`, or the OS keychain — especially for `daemon serve`, which is long-running

      --token-secret-file <PATH>
          Read the API token secret from a file (audit #283).
          
          Unlike `--token-secret` this leaves nothing in the process listing. The file must be readable by this process; trailing whitespace is trimmed. Takes precedence over `--token-secret` when both are given, because it is the safer of the two and a caller passing both most likely means the file.

      --secure
          Require Telegram 2FA for destructive operations in the TUI; refuse destructive CLI commands (exit 8)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== proxxx accounting --help =====
Per-pool / per-node / per-tag resource accounting. Current state only — time-window aggregation deferred to a follow-up

Usage: proxxx accounting [OPTIONS]

Options:
      --group-by <GROUP_BY>
          Dimension to aggregate on. Defaults to `pool` — the most common chargeback question

          Possible values:
          - pool: Resource pool (PVE `/pool/<name>`)
          - node: Cluster node
          - tag:  Free-form tag — guests have a CSV `tags` field; each tag becomes its own row (a guest with `prod,critical` contributes to two groups)
          
          [default: pool]

      --include-unassigned
          Include guests with no group assignment under a synthetic `(none)` row. Off by default — operators usually want to see assigned pools only

      --timeframe <TIMEFRAME>
          Aggregate over a historical time window via per-guest RRD. `none` (default) emits current-state totals only. Anything else adds CPU-hours, GiB-hours, and per-direction network GiB columns by walking PVE's RRD samples for each guest.
          
          One RRD call per guest — expect 30-60 s on a 100-guest cluster with `--timeframe month`.

          Possible values:
          - none:  Current-state only — no per-guest RRD fetch
          - hour
          - day
          - week
          - month
          - year
          
          [default: none]

      --output <OUTPUT>
          [default: text]
          [possible values: text, json]

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Connection profile name

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format (only in CLI mode)

          Possible values:
          - json
          - json-envelope: JSON wrapped in a envelope carrying the producing version (audit 2026-09-09, #284)
          - table
          - plain
          
          [default: table]

      --token-secret <TOKEN_SECRET>
          API token secret. **Visible in the process listing** (`ps`, `/proc/<pid>/cmdline`) for as long as the command runs, and in shell history and CI logs. Prefer `--token-secret-file`, `PROXXX_TOKEN_SECRET`, or the OS keychain — especially for `daemon serve`, which is long-running

      --token-secret-file <PATH>
          Read the API token secret from a file (audit #283).
          
          Unlike `--token-secret` this leaves nothing in the process listing. The file must be readable by this process; trailing whitespace is trimmed. Takes precedence over `--token-secret` when both are given, because it is the safer of the two and a caller passing both most likely means the file.

      --secure
          Require Telegram 2FA for destructive operations in the TUI; refuse destructive CLI commands (exit 8)

