  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== proxxx clone --help =====
Clone a guest into a new VMID. The source can be a template (canonical IAC pattern) or a regular guest. For QEMU, `--full` produces an independent disk copy; without it, a linked clone backed by the source's base disk (fast, requires source to be a template). LXC clones are always full

Usage: proxxx clone [OPTIONS] <SRC_VMID>

Arguments:
  <SRC_VMID>
          Source guest VMID

Options:
      --newid <NEWID>
          Target VMID. If omitted, fetched from `GET /cluster/nextid`

      --name <NAME>
          Display name (QEMU) or hostname (LXC) of the new guest

      --target <TARGET>
          Target node. Defaults to the source node

      --storage <STORAGE>
          Target storage. Required for cross-storage full clones

      --full
          Full clone (independent copy). Linked clone otherwise. LXC ignores this — clones are always full for containers

      --snapname <SNAPNAME>
          Clone from a specific snapshot rather than running state

      --description <DESCRIPTION>
          Description for the new guest

      --cloud-init-user <FILE>
          Cloud-init customization TOML file. Applied (and the cloud-init drive regenerated) after the clone task completes. QEMU-only. Supported keys: ciuser, cipassword, sshkey, `sshkey_file`, ipconfig0, searchdomain, nameserver

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Connection profile name

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format (only in CLI mode)

          Possible values:
          - json
          - json-envelope: JSON wrapped in a envelope carrying the producing version (audit 2026-09-09, #284)
          - table
          - plain
          
          [default: table]

      --token-secret <TOKEN_SECRET>
          API token secret. **Visible in the process listing** (`ps`, `/proc/<pid>/cmdline`) for as long as the command runs, and in shell history and CI logs. Prefer `--token-secret-file`, `PROXXX_TOKEN_SECRET`, or the OS keychain — especially for `daemon serve`, which is long-running

      --token-secret-file <PATH>
          Read the API token secret from a file (audit #283).
          
          Unlike `--token-secret` this leaves nothing in the process listing. The file must be readable by this process; trailing whitespace is trimmed. Takes precedence over `--token-secret` when both are given, because it is the safer of the two and a caller passing both most likely means the file.

      --secure
          Require Telegram 2FA for destructive operations in the TUI; refuse destructive CLI commands (exit 8)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

