===== proxxx pbs restore --help =====
Restore an archive to a local target directory. By default the whole archive is extracted; pass one or more `--pattern` globs to restore only matching files/subdirs (single-file restore), e.g. `--pattern etc/network/interfaces`. Matched files land under `--target` preserving their in-archive path

Usage: proxxx pbs restore [OPTIONS] --store <STORE> --snapshot <SNAPSHOT> --archive <ARCHIVE> --target <TARGET>

Options:
      --store <STORE>
          

      --snapshot <SNAPSHOT>
          Snapshot reference, e.g. `vm/100/2024-01-15T10:00:00Z`

      --archive <ARCHIVE>
          Archive name, e.g. `root.pxar.didx`

      --target <TARGET>
          Local directory to restore into

      --pattern <PATTERN>
          Restore only files matching this glob within the archive (`proxmox-backup-client restore --pattern`). Repeatable — pass `--pattern` multiple times to select several files. Omit to restore the entire archive

      --yes
          Required: confirms this writes to the local filesystem

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Connection profile name

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format (only in CLI mode)

          Possible values:
          - json
          - json-envelope: JSON wrapped in a envelope carrying the producing version (audit 2026-09-09, #284)
          - table
          - plain
          
          [default: table]

      --token-secret <TOKEN_SECRET>
          API token secret. **Visible in the process listing** (`ps`, `/proc/<pid>/cmdline`) for as long as the command runs, and in shell history and CI logs. Prefer `--token-secret-file`, `PROXXX_TOKEN_SECRET`, or the OS keychain — especially for `daemon serve`, which is long-running

      --token-secret-file <PATH>
          Read the API token secret from a file (audit #283).
          
          Unlike `--token-secret` this leaves nothing in the process listing. The file must be readable by this process; trailing whitespace is trimmed. Takes precedence over `--token-secret` when both are given, because it is the safer of the two and a caller passing both most likely means the file.

      --secure
          Require Telegram 2FA for destructive operations in the TUI; refuse destructive CLI commands (exit 8)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== proxxx ha --help =====
HA + replication console (read-only inspector)

Usage: proxxx ha [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  groups          List HA groups (PVE-version-tolerant: hits `/cluster/ha/rules` on PVE 9, falls back to `/cluster/ha/groups` semantics)
  groups-legacy   Hit the LITERAL `/cluster/ha/groups` path (PVE 8 only — on PVE 9 this returns 500 because the path was migrated to `/cluster/ha/rules`)
  group-create    Create an HA group (PVE 8). Nodes are CSV with optional `:priority` suffixes, e.g. `pve1:2,pve2:1,pve3`
  group-update    Update an HA group's nodes / restricted / nofailback / comment
  group-delete    Delete an HA group. Refuses unless `--yes` is passed
  resources       List HA-managed resources (VMs/CTs)
  status          Show the HA manager runtime status (raw CRM internal state)
  status-current  User-facing live HA status — heterogeneous list mixing per-node, per-service, and master/quorum rows. Higher-level than `status`
  preview         "What if?" preview: where does each resource land if a node fails?
  help            Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --profile <PROFILE>
          Connection profile name

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format (only in CLI mode)

          Possible values:
          - json
          - json-envelope: JSON wrapped in a envelope carrying the producing version (audit 2026-09-09, #284)
          - table
          - plain
          
          [default: table]

      --token-secret <TOKEN_SECRET>
          API token secret. **Visible in the process listing** (`ps`, `/proc/<pid>/cmdline`) for as long as the command runs, and in shell history and CI logs. Prefer `--token-secret-file`, `PROXXX_TOKEN_SECRET`, or the OS keychain — especially for `daemon serve`, which is long-running

      --token-secret-file <PATH>
          Read the API token secret from a file (audit #283).
          
          Unlike `--token-secret` this leaves nothing in the process listing. The file must be readable by this process; trailing whitespace is trimmed. Takes precedence over `--token-secret` when both are given, because it is the safer of the two and a caller passing both most likely means the file.

      --secure
          Require Telegram 2FA for destructive operations in the TUI; refuse destructive CLI commands (exit 8)

