===== proxxx metrics --help =====
Time-series metrics (rrddata): historical CPU/mem/disk/net for guests, nodes, and storages. Default output is a Unicode block sparkline + min/max/avg summary; `--format json` emits raw rrddata for piping into jq / a charting tool

Usage: proxxx metrics [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  vm       Per-VM metrics. Auto-discovers which node owns the VMID unless `--node` is supplied (faster — skips the cluster-wide scan)
  ct       Per-LXC metrics
  node     Per-node metrics. Adds `loadavg`, `iowait`, root pool usage on top of the guest fields
  storage  Per-storage capacity metrics (`used` / `total`)
  serve    Start a Prometheus metrics exporter. Scrapes nodes, guests and storage from the PVE API on every pull and serves them in Prometheus text format on GET /metrics. No auth — bind to localhost and put a reverse proxy or firewall in front for production deployments
  rrd-png  Pre-rendered PNG graph reference (server-side path) for a guest. Distinct from `vm`/`ct` which return numeric series — this is for UI/export pipelines wanting an existing image
  help     Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --profile <PROFILE>
          Connection profile name

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format (only in CLI mode)

          Possible values:
          - json
          - json-envelope: JSON wrapped in a envelope carrying the producing version (audit 2026-09-09, #284)
          - table
          - plain
          
          [default: table]

      --token-secret <TOKEN_SECRET>
          API token secret. **Visible in the process listing** (`ps`, `/proc/<pid>/cmdline`) for as long as the command runs, and in shell history and CI logs. Prefer `--token-secret-file`, `PROXXX_TOKEN_SECRET`, or the OS keychain — especially for `daemon serve`, which is long-running

      --token-secret-file <PATH>
          Read the API token secret from a file (audit #283).
          
          Unlike `--token-secret` this leaves nothing in the process listing. The file must be readable by this process; trailing whitespace is trimmed. Takes precedence over `--token-secret` when both are given, because it is the safer of the two and a caller passing both most likely means the file.

      --secure
          Require Telegram 2FA for destructive operations in the TUI; refuse destructive CLI commands (exit 8)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

===== proxxx metrics vm --help =====
Per-VM metrics. Auto-discovers which node owns the VMID unless `--node` is supplied (faster — skips the cluster-wide scan)

Usage: proxxx metrics vm [OPTIONS] <VMID>

Arguments:
  <VMID>
          

Options:
      --node <NODE>
          

      --field <FIELD>
          Possible values:
          - cpu:       CPU utilisation (0.0..1.0; multiply by 100 for percentage)
          - mem:       Memory used (bytes). For node, falls back to `memused` when `mem` is absent (PVE node responses use the verbose name)
          - diskread:  Bytes read from disk in this bucket
          - diskwrite: Bytes written to disk in this bucket
          - netin:     Bytes received on network
          - netout:    Bytes sent on network
          - loadavg:   Load average (node-only)
          - iowait:    IO-wait fraction (node-only)
          - used:      Capacity used. Storage = `used`; guest = `disk`
          - total:     Total capacity. Storage = `total`; guest = `maxdisk`
          
          [default: cpu]

      --timeframe <TIMEFRAME>
          [default: hour]
          [possible values: hour, day, week, month, year]

      --cf <CF>
          [default: average]
          [possible values: average, max]

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Connection profile name

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format (only in CLI mode)

          Possible values:
          - json
          - json-envelope: JSON wrapped in a envelope carrying the producing version (audit 2026-09-09, #284)
          - table
          - plain
          
          [default: table]

      --token-secret <TOKEN_SECRET>
          API token secret. **Visible in the process listing** (`ps`, `/proc/<pid>/cmdline`) for as long as the command runs, and in shell history and CI logs. Prefer `--token-secret-file`, `PROXXX_TOKEN_SECRET`, or the OS keychain — especially for `daemon serve`, which is long-running

      --token-secret-file <PATH>
          Read the API token secret from a file (audit #283).
          
          Unlike `--token-secret` this leaves nothing in the process listing. The file must be readable by this process; trailing whitespace is trimmed. Takes precedence over `--token-secret` when both are given, because it is the safer of the two and a caller passing both most likely means the file.

