===== proxxx node-system --help =====
Node system layer — DNS resolvers, /etc/hosts, NTP, journal, syslog, subscription, certificates, support report, wake-on-LAN. One command per node, sub-commands per resource

Usage: proxxx node-system [OPTIONS] <NODE> <COMMAND>

Commands:
  dns           DNS resolver config (search domain + up to 3 nameservers)
  hosts         `/etc/hosts` content + digest-guarded atomic replace
  journal       Tail systemd journal with PVE filters. The systemd journal. To filter by unit, use `syslog --service`; PVE's journal endpoint has no unit filter
  syslog        Page through the node's syslog, oldest line first
  time          NTP / timezone — `get` reports clock + zone, `set` updates timezone (clock itself is NTP-driven)
  wol           Wake the node from S5/standby via cluster-network magic packet
  subscription  Subscription state + key management
  cert          pveproxy TLS certificates — list, upload custom, delete custom, order ACME
  report        `pvereport` support bundle (plain text, many KB)
  help          Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Arguments:
  <NODE>
          Target node name (e.g. `pve1`)

Options:
      --profile <PROFILE>
          Connection profile name

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format (only in CLI mode)

          Possible values:
          - json
          - json-envelope: JSON wrapped in a envelope carrying the producing version (audit 2026-09-09, #284)
          - table
          - plain
          
          [default: table]

      --token-secret <TOKEN_SECRET>
          API token secret. **Visible in the process listing** (`ps`, `/proc/<pid>/cmdline`) for as long as the command runs, and in shell history and CI logs. Prefer `--token-secret-file`, `PROXXX_TOKEN_SECRET`, or the OS keychain — especially for `daemon serve`, which is long-running

      --token-secret-file <PATH>
          Read the API token secret from a file (audit #283).
          
          Unlike `--token-secret` this leaves nothing in the process listing. The file must be readable by this process; trailing whitespace is trimmed. Takes precedence over `--token-secret` when both are given, because it is the safer of the two and a caller passing both most likely means the file.

      --secure
          Require Telegram 2FA for destructive operations in the TUI; refuse destructive CLI commands (exit 8)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

