===== proxxx storage-defs create --help =====
Create a new storage. PVE validates type-specific required fields inline (e.g. `nfs` requires `--server` + `--export`; `pbs` requires `--server` + `--datastore`)

Usage: proxxx storage-defs create [OPTIONS] --storage <STORAGE> --storage-type <TYPE>

Options:
      --storage <STORAGE>
          Storage id (operator-chosen name)

      --storage-type <TYPE>
          Storage type: `dir` | `lvm` | `lvmthin` | `zfspool` | `nfs` | `cifs` | `iscsi` | `glusterfs` | `cephfs` | `rbd` | `pbs` | `btrfs` | `esxi`

      --content <CONTENT>
          CSV: `vztmpl,iso,backup,images,rootdir,snippets`

      --nodes <NODES>
          CSV node-restriction. Empty = all nodes (default)

      --shared <SHARED>
          1 = visible to every node (NFS, PBS, `CephFS`, etc.)
          
          [possible values: true, false]

      --path <PATH>
          `dir` / `btrfs`: filesystem path

      --server <SERVER>
          `nfs` / `cifs` / `pbs` / `iscsi`: server hostname/IP

      --export <EXPORT>
          `nfs`: export path on the server

      --datastore <DATASTORE>
          `pbs`: datastore name

      --fingerprint <FINGERPRINT>
          `pbs` / `cifs`: TLS fingerprint for verification

      --username <USERNAME>
          `cifs` / `pbs`: auth username

      --pool <POOL>
          `zfspool` / `rbd`: ZFS dataset / Ceph pool name

      --vgname <VGNAME>
          `lvm` / `lvmthin`: volume group name

      --thinpool <THINPOOL>
          `lvmthin`: thin pool name within `vgname`

      --raw <RAW>
          

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Connection profile name

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format (only in CLI mode)

          Possible values:
          - json
          - json-envelope: JSON wrapped in a envelope carrying the producing version (audit 2026-09-09, #284)
          - table
          - plain
          
          [default: table]

      --token-secret <TOKEN_SECRET>
          API token secret. **Visible in the process listing** (`ps`, `/proc/<pid>/cmdline`) for as long as the command runs, and in shell history and CI logs. Prefer `--token-secret-file`, `PROXXX_TOKEN_SECRET`, or the OS keychain — especially for `daemon serve`, which is long-running

      --token-secret-file <PATH>
          Read the API token secret from a file (audit #283).
          
          Unlike `--token-secret` this leaves nothing in the process listing. The file must be readable by this process; trailing whitespace is trimmed. Takes precedence over `--token-secret` when both are given, because it is the safer of the two and a caller passing both most likely means the file.

      --secure
          Require Telegram 2FA for destructive operations in the TUI; refuse destructive CLI commands (exit 8)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

