===== proxxx access --help =====
Access control: ACL, users, groups, roles, realms, TFA

Usage: proxxx access [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  acl           List ACL entries
  users         List users
  groups        List groups
  roles         List roles (with their privileges)
  realms        List authentication realms (PAM/PVE/AD/LDAP/OIDC)
  tfa           List TFA entries for a user
  user-create   Create a user. `userid` must include the realm (e.g. `alice@pve`, `svc@pam`)
  user-update   Modify an existing user. Only the fields you pass are changed
  user-delete   Delete a user. PVE refuses if the user owns API tokens — revoke those first via `proxxx token revoke`
  group-create  Create a group
  group-delete  Delete a group. PVE refuses if any user is still a member — remove members first via `proxxx access user-update --groups <new-csv>`
  acl-set       Grant a role to a user/group/token on a path
  acl-unset     Revoke a role from a user/group/token on a path
  permissions   Effective permissions tree for a user (or self) on a path (or all paths). Hits `/access/permissions` directly — no SSH dependency, unlike the `proxxx perms` shellout
  password      Change a user's password. Requires either being the user themselves, or `Realm.AllocateUser` on `/access/{realm}` (typically root@pam)
  help          Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --profile <PROFILE>
          Connection profile name

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format (only in CLI mode)

          Possible values:
          - json
          - json-envelope: JSON wrapped in a envelope carrying the producing version (audit 2026-09-09, #284)
          - table
          - plain
          
          [default: table]

      --token-secret <TOKEN_SECRET>
          API token secret. **Visible in the process listing** (`ps`, `/proc/<pid>/cmdline`) for as long as the command runs, and in shell history and CI logs. Prefer `--token-secret-file`, `PROXXX_TOKEN_SECRET`, or the OS keychain — especially for `daemon serve`, which is long-running

      --token-secret-file <PATH>
          Read the API token secret from a file (audit #283).
          
          Unlike `--token-secret` this leaves nothing in the process listing. The file must be readable by this process; trailing whitespace is trimmed. Takes precedence over `--token-secret` when both are given, because it is the safer of the two and a caller passing both most likely means the file.

      --secure
          Require Telegram 2FA for destructive operations in the TUI; refuse destructive CLI commands (exit 8)

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

