===== proxxx reconcile converge --help =====
Converge the live cluster toward a desired-state source — the WRITE half of reconcile. Resolves drift exactly like `reconcile run`, then applies it with the same pre-flight safety gate as `state apply`. Always rehearse with `--dry-run` first.

Examples: proxxx reconcile converge --source ./state.toml --dry-run       # preview proxxx reconcile converge --source git@host:cluster.git          # apply, no deletes proxxx reconcile converge --source <src> --prune                 # apply + delete drift proxxx reconcile converge --source <src> --prune --interactive   # prompt per Severe proxxx reconcile converge --source <src> --prune --allow-risk    # bypass gate (CI)

Usage: proxxx reconcile converge [OPTIONS] --source <SOURCE>

Options:
      --source <SOURCE>
          Desired-state source: a local file, a local directory, or a git URL (shallow-cloned each run). Same semantics as `reconcile run`

      --path <PATH>
          State file path within a directory / git-repo source. Ignored when `--source` points directly at a file
          
          [default: state.toml]

      --dry-run
          Preview only — never mutates. Every change reports as `skipped (dry_run)`. Run this first on any new source

      --prune
          Required to execute `delete` changes. Without it, deletes report as `skipped (prune_policy)` — a safety interlock, opt in deliberately

      --continue-on-error
          Don't halt on the first failure; attempt every change in order. Risky if changes have ordering dependencies

      --allow-risk
          Override the pre-flight Severe-risk gate (exit 6 otherwise). Pass only when the operator has reviewed the diff

      --interactive
          Prompt `[y/N]` on stdin for every Severe-risk change. Pair with `--allow-risk`. No stdin in CI — don't use it there

      --allow-profile-mismatch
          Allow converging toward a state file whose `[meta] profile` differs from the resolved target profile (`#346`). Without this flag, a profile mismatch refuses with exit code 3 before touching PVE

      --json
          Emit the apply outcomes as a JSON array instead of text lines

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Connection profile name

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format (only in CLI mode)

          Possible values:
          - json
          - json-envelope: JSON wrapped in a envelope carrying the producing version (audit 2026-09-09, #284)
          - table
          - plain
          
          [default: table]

      --token-secret <TOKEN_SECRET>
          API token secret. **Visible in the process listing** (`ps`, `/proc/<pid>/cmdline`) for as long as the command runs, and in shell history and CI logs. Prefer `--token-secret-file`, `PROXXX_TOKEN_SECRET`, or the OS keychain — especially for `daemon serve`, which is long-running

      --token-secret-file <PATH>
          Read the API token secret from a file (audit #283).
          
          Unlike `--token-secret` this leaves nothing in the process listing. The file must be readable by this process; trailing whitespace is trimmed. Takes precedence over `--token-secret` when both are given, because it is the safer of the two and a caller passing both most likely means the file.

      --secure
